Kubernetes deployments

forget about pods, replicasets and other things

how you want to deploy your application in a production environment,
 - you need webserver that needs to be deployed in prod env
 - not one but many such instances runing for reasons
 - whenever newer versions of application builds(image) become available (may be on docker registry) you would like to upgrade your docker instances seamlessly
 - you do not want to upgrade all of them at once, coz this may impact user accessing application
 - you upgrade them one after other, that kind of upgrade is known a rolling updates
 - suppose one of the upgrade you performed resulted in unexpected error and you are asked to undo the recent change
 - you would like to roll back the changes you carried out

 - you would like to make multiple changes to your environment
 such as upgrading underlying web server versions, scaling your environment, modifying resource allocation etc
 - you dont want apply each change immediately
 - instead you would like to apply a path to your environment, make the changes and resume so that all the changes are rolled out together

 all of these capabilities are available in kubernetes deployments

 pod deploys single instance of our application
 each container is encapsulated inside pod

 multiple such pods are deployed using replicasets or replication controller

 and then deployment, which is higher in the hierarchy , it is a high level object  
 it provides the capability to upgrade the underlying instances seamlessly using rolling updates and pause and resume changes as required

   deployment
        |
   replicaset
   |    |    |
 pod1 pod2  pod3

--------------

how do we create a deployment

we create a deployment definition file
the contents of the deployment definition file are similar to replica set definition file, except for the kind, now it is going to be Deployment

apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  #deployment name
  name: myapp-deployment
  labels:
    app: myapp
    type: frontend

spec:
  template: 
    metadata:
      name: myapp-pod
      labels:
        app: myapp
        type: frontend
    spec: 
      containers:
        - name: nginx
          image: nginx
replicas=5
selector:
  matchLabels:
    app: myapp
    type: frontend

#once the file is ready run
kubectl create -f deployment-definition.yaml


#to view deployments
kubectl get deployments
myapp-deployment

#the deployment automatically creates replicaset in the name of deployment
kubectl get replicaset
myapp-deployment-6799874646

#replicaset ultimately create pods
kubectl get pods
myapp-deployment-6799874646-54hr5

# to see all the created objects(deployments, deplicasets, pods,etc)
kubectl get all 